module std.nat


// A natural number is a CARRIER, not an operations table. The prior declaration
// -- Nat = CommutativeSemiring<Magnitude> -- said a natural number IS a record of
// {add, zero, mul, one}, naming the STRUCTURE where the carrier belonged. Its own
// module contradicted it: nat_compare and nat_max order two Nats with `<` and `>`,
// which no operations-record admits. The import era never made a consumer check
// the declaration, so the contradiction sat unobserved rather than being allowed.
//
// The semiring is a VALUE the carrier inhabits (v2.std.nat models it that way, and
// DESIGN §4 states the rule: operations come from inhabitance, as Int.add derives
// from Int inhabiting a ring). No such value is declared here because nothing in
// the corpus consumes a Nat's .add/.zero/.mul/.one -- adding one now would be
// speculative structure, not grounding.
//
// `Int where range(min: 0)` is the tree's existing idiom for exactly this shape:
// std.types declares EpochSecs, EpochMs and four others the same way.
type Nat = Int where range(min: 0)

fn nat_compare(a: Nat, b: Nat) -> Ordering {
  if a < b { Less } else { if a > b { Greater } else { Equal } }
}

fn nat_max(a: Nat, b: Nat) -> Nat {
  if a > b { a } else { b }
}

fn nat_min(a: Nat, b: Nat) -> Nat {
  if a < b { a } else { b }
}
